  <h1>Why Did Qin Shi Huang Build the Terracotta Army?</h1>
  <div class="zh">兵马俑</div>
  <p>Qin Shi Huang, the first emperor of a unified China, commissioned the Terracotta Army as funerary art for his tomb: life-size clay warriors, horses, and chariots meant to guard him after death. Historians interpret the project as part of his quest for immortality, with a vast underground city built for the afterlife. The figures are sculptures, not buried living soldiers, and they were usually arranged in military formation.</p>
  <p style="color:#6a6153;font-size:13px;margin-top:10px">By <a href="/editorial-policy" style="color:inherit">yaopulife Editorial</a> · Published 2026-08-28</p>
</div></header>

<main class="wrap">
<section class="sec">
  <h2>What is the Terracotta Army (兵马俑)?</h2>
  <p>The <b>Terracotta Army</b> is known in Chinese as <b>兵马俑</b> (<b>Bīngmǎyǒng</b>). <b>兵</b> (bīng) means troops or soldiers, <b>马</b> (mǎ) means horses, and <b>俑</b> (yǒng) means a figurine made to be buried with the dead. In plain English: clay warriors and horses placed in a tomb, not toys and not a living army.</p>
  <p>It is a collection of life-size terracotta sculptures depicting the armies of <b>Qin Shi Huang</b> (秦始皇, Qín Shǐ Huáng; also called Shihuangdi), the first emperor of China. Accessible reference works describe the figures as <b>funerary art</b>—objects made for burial—found in his tomb complex and interred for more than 2,000 years.</p>
  <p>A useful Western comparison is an Egyptian royal tomb stocked with goods and guardian figures for the next world. The Qin project is military in scale: an underground army rather than a household of servants. A common misunderstanding is that these were real men buried alive; the surviving objects are clay sculptures.</p>
</section>
<section class="sec">
  <h2>Why did Qin Shi Huang build it?</h2>
  <p>The reason usually given is funerary. Qin Shi Huang had a vast tomb built for himself, and the terracotta figures were meant to stand as his army after death—warriors, infantrymen, horses, and related equipment—guarding an underground city.</p>
  <p>According to some historical interpretations, he may have been seeking immortality, and the mausoleum was part of that quest. That is an interpretation of motive, not a laboratory fact and not a claim that the clay army actually made him immortal. Ancient royal burials in China often included models of people, animals, and goods; this is an unusually large example of that practice, not a confirmed scene from a single eyewitness diary.</p>
  <p>The pits lie east of the Qin mausoleum near Xiyang Village in Lintong, Shaanxi. Construction is usually said to have begun before Qin unified China and to have stopped in 209 BCE. That long timeline supports reading the army as a state-scale tomb project, not a last-minute grave marker.</p>
</section>
<section class="sec">
  <h2>What was placed in the pits?</h2>
  <p>The figures were arranged in a <b>military formation</b> and were once painted with mineral colors. The gray clay look in many photographs is usually a later appearance after paint was lost. Life-size warriors and horses are the famous core; nearby chambers held more of the underground complex.</p>
  <p>Four pits have been found. The sculptures are usually described as following the organization of the Qin army. One commonly cited inventory speaks of over 7,000 warriors, about 600 horses, and about 100 chariots; treat those numbers as a widely repeated count, not a figure every source measures the same way.</p>
  <p>Think of the pits less as a sculpture gallery and more as a frozen parade ground underground: ranks meant to function, in tomb logic, as an army for the emperor.</p>
</section>
<section class="sec">
  <h2>How this compares with Western tomb stories</h2>
  <p>Western readers often map <b>兵马俑</b> onto the Egyptian pyramids: a famous tomb, a powerful king, a wonder built for the afterlife. That comparison helps at the level of royal burial. It misleads if it suggests mummies, a single pyramid chamber, or the Egyptian practice of wrapping the body.</p>
  <p>Another mismatch is the English word <b>terracotta</b>. In a Western art class it can mean a small clay sketch. Here the figures are life-size military sculptures. The Chinese term <b>俑</b> already names the job: they are burial figurines, closer in function to tomb attendants than to decorative garden statues.</p>
  <p>A closer (still imperfect) comparison is Egyptian *ushabti*—small servant figures meant to work for the dead—scaled up into a full army. The Qin version is not a set of miniatures; it is an imperial host in clay.</p>
</section>
<section class="sec">
  <h2>How to describe it without overclaiming</h2>
  <p>Present <b>兵马俑</b> as clay funerary soldiers and horses from the tomb of China's first emperor, built to guard him after death. Say <b>Qin Shi Huang</b> (秦始皇, Qín Shǐ Huáng) on first mention. Keep the immortality quest in the realm of a common interpretation.</p>
  <p>Do not call them the emperor's real army sealed alive, a stone army, or toy soldiers. Do not treat any one troop total as sacred. Folk and popular tellings vary; scholarly summaries also vary in how much they stress immortality versus general afterlife protection.</p>
  <p>The honest short version: a first emperor, a huge tomb, and a clay army meant to serve him when he was no longer among the living.</p>
</section>

